#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128911 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a453400-6363-5782-b346-aae37570c9bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Complex Eigenvalues of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "3958f8c6-0808-510b-81f5-bb82de6a203f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5904" :question/problem "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2(-2) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=1\\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128912 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eae4328-dd7c-5f77-8958-840cadb6f7f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a 2x2 Matrix Given Another Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "2c47a76d-1b5d-5a10-a3c8-aff9647ca068" :question/math-academy-id "e-5905" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_2$ are eigenvalues of a real $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find $2(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda}_1=-2-3\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}) & =2(-2+3\\text{i}+(-2-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =2(-4) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a453400-6363-5782-b346-aae37570c9bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Complex Eigenvalues of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fcc6767-ee53-58ad-936c-e4e5cc91bfe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307622} {:answer/id #uuid "9747391f-db12-5323-a421-08bcde48fac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307623} {:answer/id #uuid "79bfcf49-cc8b-5a65-9cc9-6246000568bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307624} {:answer/id #uuid "80e7a4b1-6594-5940-8b30-a1d0f2445cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307625} {:answer/id #uuid "e386df89-ab56-5b77-be86-36c4e93dc4ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fcc6767-ee53-58ad-936c-e4e5cc91bfe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307622} :answer-field/id #uuid "b45e9fc6-8508-515b-abfe-39189352afb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66c11564-3613-5dd6-9e5c-9419ee617ca1" :question/math-academy-id "q-102773" :question/problem "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-1(-2) & =0 \\\\ 4-4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(1)(6)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-8}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eae4328-dd7c-5f77-8958-840cadb6f7f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a 2x2 Matrix Given Another Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fae2e6-14c0-516b-8444-68f750cb6527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307629} {:answer/id #uuid "a88bfe7d-995e-567a-8e50-3ff8c9545133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307630} {:answer/id #uuid "cc971f74-2251-53ae-b45f-03a1bf10e715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307631} {:answer/id #uuid "73607b48-c462-53de-86b6-5fa72660e878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307632} {:answer/id #uuid "1fde457c-cb7f-5542-997b-89d29ab7765c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73607b48-c462-53de-86b6-5fa72660e878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307632} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2448746-ac4d-529f-a34e-70f6afb968f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f1195eb-a99c-59d9-bfbf-e214bc1dc200" :question/math-academy-id "q-102775" :question/problem "Given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+3\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}$ are eigenvalues of a real $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, find $\\frac{2}{3}({\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}=5-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}({\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}) & =\\frac{2}{3}(5+3\\text{i}-(5-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(6\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eae4328-dd7c-5f77-8958-840cadb6f7f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a 2x2 Matrix Given Another Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89c2f557-cb7e-5a7b-8091-d9cfde810fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307636} {:answer/id #uuid "e190183d-abfa-5662-b490-285076dc6648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307637} {:answer/id #uuid "a66c47d4-b9b4-5605-bd45-e9b8849ca086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307638} {:answer/id #uuid "3a340b43-f7c0-5e21-9d19-9745149cde37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.ty